From c8510ae232af8f4d1d21338931621654db91939b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:14:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep locales, time zones, calendars and decimals in keyed
 archives as macOS does

A formatter's keyed archive holds its locale, time zone and calendar,
its minimum and maximum (decimal numbers) and its rounding behaviour.
GNUstep archived each of these the unkeyed way even in a keyed archive,
under generated keys, so macOS could read none of them and GNUstep none
of macOS's: on macOS -[NSLocale initWithCoder:] refused a GNUstep
archive, and on GNUstep a 10.0 number formatter from macOS failed with
"no class for name 'NSDecimalNumberPlaceholder'".  Each now uses macOS's
keys in a keyed archive, and still reads the generated keys of older
GNUstep archives:

- NSLocale: NS.identifier.
- NSTimeZone: NS.name, and NS.data with the zone's data where it has
  any; a zone is read by its name.
- NSCalendar: NS.identifier, NS.locale, NS.timezone, NS.firstwkdy and
  NS.mindays.
- NSDecimalNumber: NS.mantissa (128 bits as eight 16 bit words, least
  significant first, in the byte order NS.mantissa.bo gives), NS.length
  (the words in use), NS.exponent, NS.negative and NS.compact; NaN is an
  empty negative mantissa.
- NSDecimalNumberHandler conforms to NSCoding: NS.roundingmode, NS.scale
  (left out for no scale), and each NS.raise.* that is set.

NSKeyedUnarchiver now tries the classes an archive lists in $classes
when the class named is missing, as macOS does for the fallbacks of
+classFallbacksForKeyedArchiver; macOS archives a decimal number as
NSDecimalNumberPlaceholder, a class GNUstep does not have.

Tests/base/NSKeyedArchiver/macosValueClasses.m reads archives macOS 15
wrote (macos-*.plist) and checks the keys GNUstep writes; 20 of its 25
checks fail without the change.  NSNumberFormatter/macosArchive.m and
NSDateFormatter/macosArchive.m read formatters archived on macOS, which
also needs the change reading NS.attributes; without this one they fail
at the first check.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
